  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Use the quotient rule to find $f'(x)$ and hence show that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p18-q17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{2x}{x-3}$ is decreasing on each of the intervals $x<3$ and $x>3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=-\\dfrac{6}{(x-3)^2}<0$ for $x\\ne3$, so $f$ is decreasing on each of $(-\\infty,3)$ and $(3,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{(x-3)(2)-(2x)(1)}{(x-3)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{2x-6-2x}{(x-3)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=-\\frac{6}{(x-3)^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x\\ne3$, $(x-3)^2>0$, so $f'(x)<0$. Therefore $f$ is decreasing on each of $(-\\infty,3)$ and $(3,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q17-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2+1}$ is increasing for all $x$, except $x=0$ where it is stationary.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{x^2(x^2+3)}{(x^2+1)^2}>0$ for $x\\ne0$, and $f'(0)=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{(x^2+1)(3x^2)-x^3(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{3x^4+3x^2-2x^4}{(x^2+1)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{x^4+3x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{x^2(x^2+3)}{(x^2+1)^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x\\ne0$, $x^2>0$, $x^2+3>0$ and $(x^2+1)^2>0$, so $f'(x)>0$. At $x=0$, $f'(0)=0$. Thus the curve is increasing where $x\\ne0$ and stationary at $x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 37
      }
    ]
  },
